  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Which of the following matrices are block upper triangular?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "A block upper triangular matrix can be partitioned so that it has only square blocks on the main diagonal, and the blocks below the main diagonal contain only zeros.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$is *not* a block upper triangular matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $B$ is *not* a block upper triangular matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $C$ is a block upper triangular matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\"",
